table(d,["Layer","Rev 3 direct","PERT low / mode / high","Basis for the range"],rows,widths=[1.5,0.8,1.5,3.2],font=8)
para(d,f"Results. On base ranges the total is P10 ${MC['total_excl_JK']['10']/1e6:.1f}M, P50 ${MC['total_excl_JK']['50']/1e6:.1f}M, P80 ${MC['total_excl_JK']['80']/1e6:.1f}M and P90 ${MC['total_excl_JK']['90']/1e6:.1f}M, against a deterministic base of $49.7M without risk or contingency and an approval level of $57.2M with them. The reserve needed to reach P80 is ${MC['implied_P80_reserve_over_directs']/1e6:.1f}M; the estimate carries $7.6M. On wide ranges the P80 is ${MCW['total_excl_JK']['80']/1e6:.1f}M and the P80 reserve is ${MCW['implied_P80_reserve_over_directs']/1e6:.1f}M, and the probability that the total exceeds the approval level is {MCW['p_exceed_approval_excl_JK']:.0%}. The uncertainty is concentrated in three layers: the landfill platform (standard deviation ${MC['contributions']['E']/1e6:.2f}M base, ${MCW['contributions']['E']/1e6:.2f}M wide), Station 496 (${MC['contributions']['F']/1e6:.2f}M and ${MCW['contributions']['F']/1e6:.2f}M) and the owner soft costs (${MC['contributions']['H']/1e6:.2f}M and ${MCW['contributions']['H']/1e6:.2f}M). The battery contract contributes almost nothing to the spread. That is the statistical form of the whitepaper's argument: the money at risk is in the ground, the station and the process, not in the battery.")
d.add_picture(f"{B}/mc_hist.png",width=Inches(6.5)); d.add_picture(f"{B}/mc_tornado.png",width=Inches(6.5))
d.add_heading("6. Estimate-to-actual uplift on comparable projects",1)
para(d,"A second, smaller reference class answers a different question: how far have conceptual or approved figures for small utility-owned batteries moved by completion? The dataset contains five observations. BGE Fairhaven went from $9.84M estimated to $16.16M actual (+64%). Eversource Provincetown was approved at $35M to $45M and closed at $49M (+9% to +40%). Hawaiian Electric Waena moved from about $60M estimated to an $82.1M approved cap (+37%) with a further increase requested for tariffs. SaskPower Regina rose from C$26M to C$34M (+31%). Hyde Park's own September cycle moved from $47.2M to $57.2M (+21%) as the battery line caught up to the contract. Five points do not make a distribution, but their median of about +31% is consistent with Flyvbjerg's finding that fewer than half of projects finish on budget and with Sovacool and Ryu's 2025 mean of +41% across 662 electricity projects, and it is well above the +1% to +13% that those authors report for solar, wind and transmission. Against that, the 17.9% risk-plus-contingency carried on Rev 3 is not generous. It is the reserve of a project that expects to hold its number, on a site and with an interconnection scope where the comparables did not.")
d.add_heading("7. Interconnection cost references from the ISO literature",1)
para(d,"Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's interconnection cost series gives the only systematic public data on what it costs to connect storage. In ISO New England, the storage median rose from $3/kW for studies in 2010 to 2017 to $148/kW for 2018 to 2021, with an all-status mean near $230/kW; network upgrades were 3 to 31 percent of completed projects' cost and 58 percent of active ones. The June 2026 interim results of ISO-NE's Transitional Cluster Study put average interconnection cost for 23 transmission-connected projects at about $664/kW, over $1,000/kW in western Massachusetts and about $177/kW in southeastern Massachusetts, and 21 of the 26 requests were batteries; one 500 MW / 2,000 MWh Section 83E winner withdrew citing those costs. NYISO is the only ISO where LBNL reports storage cost by size: $76/kW for 1 to 50 MW requests against $32/kW above 250 MW. Hyde Park is distribution-connected and avoids the ISO queue, but the point of comparison holds: its Station 360 and Station 496 layers, loaded, are $1,374/kW, which is the cost of building a dedicated switching station and reprotecting four feeders rather than tapping an existing bus. A transmission-connected 10 MW project in ISO-NE today would face interconnection costs of the same order before it built anything of its own.")
d.add_heading("8. How to use this in the argument",1)
bullets(d,[
 ("Lead with the reference class, not the regression. ","Thirteen named projects with named costs are more persuasive to a non-statistician than a fitted line, and the placement of Hyde Park's direct cost among Fairhaven, Chateaugay and Provincetown is the single most useful fact this memo produces."),
 ("Quote the regression as a bracket. ","'Public disclosures imply $600 to $2,000 per kWh for a project of this size and duration; our direct cost is $1,483 and our approval level, which carries $23.7M of loaders that disclosures rarely include, is $2,862.' Do not quote a percentile to a regulator without the interval beside it."),
 ("Use the Monte Carlo to defend the reserve, and rebuild it with the team. ","The distributions here are the analyst's reading of the evidence. Before full funding, run a two-hour range-estimating session (AACE 41R-08) with Substation Engineering, Civil, P&C, Siting and Haugland, replace each range with the room's, and add correlation between the site, station and engineering layers. The result will be a P50 and P80 that the project team owns."),
 ("Verify the ten highest-priority public figures at source. ","Mass.gov utility-owned BESS page, the MD PSC pilot report, PGE's 2024 10-K, HEI's 10-Q, the CPUC SCE resolution, the NYPA and O&R releases, and the LBNL ISO-NE brief. Until then every external use of these numbers carries the retrieval caveat."),
 ("Keep the layered view as the frame. ","Every statistical result above is only interpretable because the estimate was first separated into layers with definitions that match the benchmarks. The statistics bolster the argument; the extraction is the argument."),
])
